Chapter: RecheckBounds
  cites Recheck chapter RecheckCore

 Stage 2. Re-derives the range of every value that enters a bounded slot
 and requires it to fit the bounds declared there.

 The derivation rules are the ones published in docs/DevelopersGuide.md
 under Static Bounds Prover, re-implemented here rather than lifted from
 the compiler's own prover: a second implementation of a published rule
 is the whole point, and an implementation transcribed from the original
 inherits the original's misreadings.

 The rechecker does NOT read a node's own type as its derived range. A
 binary node carries the type of its LEFT OPERAND, so `n * 2` for
 `n : Integer between 0 and 10` is emitted typed `(int 0 10 ov-error)`
 while the value's range is 0..20. Trusting node types here would produce
 a disagreement at almost every arithmetic node and every one of them
 would be this rechecker's own.

Section: Ranges

  RcRange = record {
   rr-lo : Integer,
   rr-hi : Integer,
   rr-known : Boolean
  }

  rc-range : Integer, Integer -> RcRange
  rc-range (lo) (hi) = RcRange { rr-lo = lo, rr-hi = hi, rr-known = True }

 This is a module-level record and so allocates on every MENTION rather
 than once, which this tree measures at the same cost as rebuilding it in
 the loop. Left that way knowingly: there is no cheaper form for a record
 value short of threading one through every derivation, the record is
 three fields, and the derivation is fuel-capped per slot. The
 list-constant form of this trap is the one that matters, and that one is
 fixed in RecheckWellFormed rather than annotated.

  rc-range-unknown : RcRange
  rc-range-unknown = RcRange { rr-lo = 0, rr-hi = 0, rr-known = False }

 Products and sums of two full-width bands overflow the arithmetic doing
 the deriving, which would answer a range narrower than the truth and turn
 this instrument into a source of false agreement. Anything wider than
 this guard is answered as unknown instead.

  rc-derive-guard : Integer = 2147483647

  rc-range-small : RcRange -> Boolean
  rc-range-small (r) =
   if r.rr-known == False then False
   else r.rr-lo > (0 - rc-derive-guard) & r.rr-hi < rc-derive-guard

  rc-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rc-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  rc-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rc-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  rc-min4 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rc-min4 (a) (b) (c) (d) = rc-min (rc-min a b) (rc-min c d)

  rc-max4 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rc-max4 (a) (b) (c) (d) = rc-max (rc-max a b) (rc-max c d)

  rc-range-union : RcRange, RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-range-union (a) (b) =
   if a.rr-known == False | b.rr-known == False then rc-range-unknown
   else rc-range (rc-min (a.rr-lo) (b.rr-lo)) (rc-max (a.rr-hi) (b.rr-hi))

 Containment is the whole judgement of this stage: a value fits a slot
 when its whole range lies inside the slot's declared band.

  rc-range-within : RcRange, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  rc-range-within (r) (lo) (hi) = lo <= r.rr-lo & r.rr-hi <= hi

Section: Let Bindings

 The published table's last row is `let x = v in body` carrying v's range
 for x in the body. Without it a bounded return written through a let
 derives from the let name's own type, which for a plain Integer is the
 full band, and the rechecker reports a disagreement that is entirely its
 own: `ret-via-let` in codex/test/mod-bound-return did exactly that.

 A binding is pushed only when its range is KNOWN, because an unknown one
 changes no answer -- the lookup falls back to the name's declared type
 either way -- and the push is what costs. Lookup runs from the most
 recent binding backwards so an inner let shadows an outer one of the
 same name.

  RcBind = record {
   rb-name : Text,
   rb-range : RcRange
  }

  rc-bind-lookup : List RcBind, Text, Integer -> RcRange
  rc-bind-lookup (bs) (n) (i) =
   if i < 0 then rc-range-unknown
   else let b = list-at bs i
   in if b.rb-name == n then b.rb-range else rc-bind-lookup bs n (i - 1)

  rc-bind-push : List RcBind, Text, RcRange -> List RcBind
  rc-bind-push (env) (n) (r) =
   if r.rr-known == False then env
   else list-push env (RcBind { rb-name = n, rb-range = r })

Section: Bounded Slots

  rc-i64-max : Integer = 9223372036854775807
  rc-i64-min : Integer = 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1

 A slot is bounded when its band is narrower than the full width. A plain
 Integer is a band too, just the widest one, and nothing flows out of it.

  rc-is-bounded-int : CodexType -> Boolean
  rc-is-bounded-int (t) =
   when t
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (m) -> lo > rc-i64-min | hi < rc-i64-max
    is otherwise -> False

  rc-int-lo : CodexType -> Integer
  rc-int-lo (t) = when t is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (m) -> lo is otherwise -> rc-i64-min

  rc-int-hi : CodexType -> Integer
  rc-int-hi (t) = when t is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (m) -> hi is otherwise -> rc-i64-max

  rc-ty-range : CodexType -> RcRange
  rc-ty-range (t) =
   when t
    is IntegerTy (lo) (hi) (m) -> rc-range lo hi
    is otherwise -> rc-range-unknown

Section: Derivation

 `__narrow` is an explicit runtime-checked assertion, so a value reaching a
 bounded slot through one is legal whatever its static range: the trap is
 the contract. It propagates the inner range for anything further up.

  rc-is-named : IRExpr, Text -> Boolean
  rc-is-named (f) (nm) =
   when f
    is IrName (n) (t) (sp) -> n == nm
    is otherwise -> False

  rc-is-narrow-call : IRExpr -> Boolean
  rc-is-narrow-call (x) =
   when x
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (sp) -> rc-is-narrow-name f
    is otherwise -> False

  rc-is-narrow-name : IRExpr -> Boolean
  rc-is-narrow-name (f) = rc-is-named f "__narrow"

  rc-derive : List RcBind, IRExpr, Integer -> RcRange
  rc-derive (env) (x) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then rc-range-unknown
   else when x
    is IrIntLit (v) (sp) -> rc-range v v
    is IrName (n) (t) (sp) -> rc-derive-name env n t
    is IrNegate (v) (t) (sp) -> rc-derive-negate (rc-derive env v (fuel - 1))
    is IrIf (c) (a) (b) (t) (sp) -> rc-range-union (rc-derive env a (fuel - 1)) (rc-derive env b (fuel - 1))
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (sp) -> rc-derive (rc-bind-push env n (rc-derive env v (fuel - 1))) b (fuel - 1)
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (sp) -> rc-ty-range t
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (sp) -> rc-derive-apply env x f a t (fuel - 1)
    is IrBinary (o) (l) (r) (t) (sp) -> rc-derive-binary o (rc-derive env l (fuel - 1)) (rc-derive env r (fuel - 1))
    is otherwise -> rc-range-unknown

 A let-bound range beats the name's declared type; a name the environment
 does not carry falls back to the type, which is what every name did
 before the environment existed.

  rc-derive-name : List RcBind, Text, CodexType -> RcRange
  rc-derive-name (env) (n) (t) =
   let b = rc-bind-lookup env n (list-length env - 1)
   in if b.rr-known then b else rc-ty-range t

  rc-derive-negate : RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-derive-negate (r) =
   if r.rr-known == False then rc-range-unknown
   else rc-range (0 - r.rr-hi) (0 - r.rr-lo)

 An application's own type field IS its result type, unlike a binary
 node's, so a call's range is read from the callee's declared return.

  rc-derive-apply : List RcBind, IRExpr, IRExpr, IRExpr, CodexType, Integer -> RcRange
  rc-derive-apply (env) (whole) (f) (a) (t) (fuel) =
   if rc-is-narrow-name f then rc-derive env a fuel
   else if rc-is-int-mod-partial f then rc-derive-int-mod (rc-derive env a fuel)
   else rc-ty-range t

 `int-mod x n` for n > 0 proves [0, n-1] -- the Euclidean remainder, never
 negative, which is the row `int-rem` does not get. A two-argument builtin
 arrives curried, so the divisor is the OUTER application's argument and
 the partially applied inner one is what names the builtin. Its result
 type on the wire is the builtin's declared plain Integer, so without this
 rule the value derives to the full band and a provable bounded return is
 reported as a disagreement.

  rc-is-int-mod-partial : IRExpr -> Boolean
  rc-is-int-mod-partial (f) =
   when f
    is IrApply (g) (x) (gt) (sp) -> rc-is-named g "int-mod"
    is otherwise -> False

 A divisor that is not provably positive answers unknown rather than a
 band that assumes the sign: the published rule is stated for n > 0 and
 nothing here may widen it.

  rc-derive-int-mod : RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-derive-int-mod (d) =
   if d.rr-known == False then rc-range-unknown
   else if d.rr-lo <= 0 then rc-range-unknown
   else rc-range 0 (d.rr-hi - 1)

  rc-derive-binary : IRBinaryOp, RcRange, RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-derive-binary (op) (a) (b) =
   if rc-range-small a == False | rc-range-small b == False then rc-range-unknown
   else when op
    is IrAddInt -> rc-range (a.rr-lo + b.rr-lo) (a.rr-hi + b.rr-hi)
    is IrSubInt -> rc-range (a.rr-lo - b.rr-hi) (a.rr-hi - b.rr-lo)
    is IrMulInt -> rc-derive-mul a b
    is IrRemInt -> rc-derive-rem b
    is otherwise -> rc-range-unknown

 Multiplication of signed bands is not monotone in either endpoint, so all
 four corner products are taken rather than the lo-lo and hi-hi pair the
 non-negative case would allow.

  rc-derive-mul : RcRange, RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-derive-mul (a) (b) =
   let p1 = a.rr-lo * b.rr-lo
   in let p2 = a.rr-lo * b.rr-hi
   in let p3 = a.rr-hi * b.rr-lo
   in let p4 = a.rr-hi * b.rr-hi
   in rc-range (rc-min4 p1 p2 p3 p4) (rc-max4 p1 p2 p3 p4)

 A truncating remainder takes the sign of the dividend and is bounded by
 the divisor's magnitude, so without knowing the dividend's sign the safe
 band is symmetric.

  rc-derive-rem : RcRange -> RcRange
  rc-derive-rem (b) =
   let m = rc-max (rc-abs (b.rr-lo)) (rc-abs (b.rr-hi))
   in if m <= 0 then rc-range-unknown
   else rc-range (0 - (m - 1)) (m - 1)

  rc-abs : Integer -> Integer
  rc-abs (n) = if n < 0 then 0 - n else n

Section: The Narrowing Sites

 Two places a value enters a bounded slot and the wire shows both: an
 argument arriving at a bounded parameter, and a body arriving at a
 declared bounded return type.

  rc-bnd-expr : RcWalk, Text, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-expr (w) (d) (x) =
   if rc-dry w then w
   else rc-bnd-go (rc-burn w) d x

  rc-bnd-go : RcWalk, Text, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-go (w) (d) (x) =
   when x
    is IrApply (f) (a) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-apply w d f a
    is IrBinary (o) (l) (r) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr w d l) d r
    is IrNegate (v) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr w d v
    is IrIf (c) (a) (b) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr w d c) d a) d b
    is IrLet (n) (t) (v) (b) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr w d v) d b
    is IrLambda (ps) (b) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr w d b
    is IrList (es) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-list w d es 0 (list-length es)
    is IrMatch (s) (bs) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-branches (rc-bnd-expr w d s) d bs 0 (list-length bs)
    is IrAct (ss) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-stmts w d ss 0 (list-length ss)
    is IrFieldAccess (r) (f) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr w d r
    is IrFieldStore (r) (f) (v) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr w d r) d v
    is IrRecord (n) (fs) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-fieldvals w d fs 0 (list-length fs)
    is IrFork (b) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr w d b
    is IrAwait (b) (t) (sp) -> rc-bnd-expr w d b
    is otherwise -> w

  rc-bnd-list : RcWalk, Text, List IRExpr, Integer, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-list (w) (d) (xs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then w
   else rc-bnd-list (rc-bnd-expr w d (list-at xs i)) d xs (i + 1) len

  rc-bnd-fieldvals : RcWalk, Text, List IRFieldVal, Integer, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-fieldvals (w) (d) (fs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then w
   else rc-bnd-fieldvals (rc-bnd-expr w d ((list-at fs i).value)) d fs (i + 1) len

  rc-bnd-branches : RcWalk, Text, List IRBranch, Integer, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-branches (w) (d) (bs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then w
   else rc-bnd-branches (rc-bnd-expr w d ((list-at bs i).body)) d bs (i + 1) len

  rc-bnd-stmts : RcWalk, Text, List IRActStmt, Integer, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-stmts (w) (d) (ss) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then w
   else rc-bnd-stmts (rc-bnd-expr w d (ir-act-stmt-expr (list-at ss i))) d ss (i + 1) len

  rc-bnd-apply : RcWalk, Text, IRExpr, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-apply (w) (d) (f) (a) =
   let w1 = rc-bnd-expr (rc-bnd-expr w d f) d a
   in rc-bnd-param-check w1 d f a

  rc-bnd-param-check : RcWalk, Text, IRExpr, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-param-check (w) (d) (f) (a) =
   when f
    is IrName (n) (ft) (sp) -> rc-bnd-against-fun w d n ft a
    is otherwise -> w

  rc-bnd-against-fun : RcWalk, Text, Text, CodexType, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-against-fun (w) (d) (n) (ft) (a) =
   when ft
    is FunTy (p) (row) (r) -> rc-bnd-slot w d ("parameter of " & n) p a
    is otherwise -> w

  rc-bnd-slot : RcWalk, Text, Text, CodexType, IRExpr -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-slot (w) (d) (site) (slot) (a) =
   if rc-is-bounded-int slot == False then w
   else if rc-is-narrow-call a then w
   else rc-bnd-verdict w d site (rc-int-lo slot) (rc-int-hi slot) (rc-derive [] a 400)

  rc-bnd-verdict : RcWalk, Text, Text, Integer, Integer, RcRange -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-verdict (w) (d) (site) (lo) (hi) (r) =
   if r.rr-known == False
   then rc-add w (rc-finding d RcUnsupported "bounds-underived" ("could not derive a range for the value reaching the " & site))
   else if rc-range-within r lo hi then w
   else rc-add w (rc-finding d RcDisagree "bounds-exceeded" ("value reaching the " & site & " has derived range " & show (r.rr-lo) & ".." & show (r.rr-hi) & " which does not fit the declared " & show lo & ".." & show hi))

Section: Definition Walk

  rc-bnd-return-ty : CodexType, Integer -> CodexType
  rc-bnd-return-ty (t) (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then t
   else when t
    is FunTy (p) (row) (r) -> rc-bnd-return-ty r (fuel - 1)
    is EffectfulTy (e) (s) (r) -> rc-bnd-return-ty r (fuel - 1)
    is otherwise -> t

  rc-bnd-def : RcWalk, IRDef -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-def (w) (d) =
   let w1 = rc-bnd-expr w (d.name) (d.body)
   in let ret = rc-bnd-return-ty (d.type-val) 32
   in if rc-is-bounded-int ret == False then w1
   else if rc-is-narrow-call (d.body) then w1
   else rc-bnd-verdict w1 (d.name) "declared return type" (rc-int-lo ret) (rc-int-hi ret) (rc-derive [] (d.body) 400)

  rc-bnd-chapter : IRChapter, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-chapter (ch) (fuel) =
   rc-bnd-defs (rc-walk-new fuel) (ch.defs) 0 (list-length (ch.defs))

  rc-bnd-defs : RcWalk, List IRDef, Integer, Integer -> RcWalk
  rc-bnd-defs (w) (ds) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then w
   else rc-bnd-defs (rc-bnd-def w (list-at ds i)) ds (i + 1) len

